        headline: "NASA's Astronomy Picture of the Day Features Stunning Total Solar Eclipse Images From Spain as Perseid Meteor Shower Reaches Maximum Activity in Pre-Dawn Hours of August 13 — International Meteor Organization Places Nominal Peak Between 02:00 and 04:00 UTC With Up to 75 Meteors Per Hour Under Moonless Skies as WB-57 Corona Dataset Returned to Johnson Space Center for Heliophysics Analysis; Astronaut Mike Fincke Retires After 30-Year Career",
        body: "NASA's Astronomy Picture of the Day for August 13 showcased spectacular imagery of yesterday's total solar eclipse captured from mainland Spain, where the Moon's shadow delivered the Iberian Peninsula's first totality since 1905 and exposed the solar corona in full glory across a path stretching from the Atlantic coast through Teruel — images that NASA heliophysics scientists are now processing alongside the multi-wavelength coronagraph data captured by the WB-57 high-altitude aircraft to assemble the most complete inner corona characterisation ever achieved for a European eclipse. The Perseid meteor shower reached its peak activity in the pre-dawn hours of August 13 as Earth crossed the densest strand of dust shed by Comet 109P/Swift-Tuttle, with the International Meteor Organization placing the nominal shower maximum between 02:00 and 04:00 UTC and international observers reporting rates of 50 to 75 meteors per hour from dark-sky sites — a performance tempered slightly compared to peak years because Jupiter's gravity has disturbed the most concentrated debris filaments ahead of a stronger predicted outburst in 2027. The rare convergence of yesterday's total solar eclipse with the annual Perseid peak drove record public engagement across NASA's skywatching outreach channels, with the agency's eclipse broadcast accumulating tens of millions of streams as the WB-57's SCIFLI Multispectral Airborne Imager dataset was successfully returned to Johnson Space Center and initial pipeline processing begun — a dataset heliophysics teams expect will underpin peer-reviewed research on coronal mass ejection source regions, helmet streamer dynamics, and inner corona magnetic topology for years to come. Veteran astronaut Mike Fincke, whose 30-year NASA career spanned four crewed spacecraft and included three spaceflights totalling 381 days in orbit, formally retired from the astronaut corps on August 12, with NASA Administrator Jared Isaacman crediting Fincke's contributions across the ISS era and Artemis transition period as among the most distinguished in the programme's modern history.",
