        body: "NASA formally invited the Indian Space Research Organisation to join the United States-led Lunar Base Programme following the conclusion of the 9th meeting of the India-US Civil Space Joint Working Group held at ISRO headquarters in Bengaluru, with the US ambassador to India officially conveying the invitation and describing India's participation as strategically vital for building a geographically diverse coalition capable of sustaining long-duration human presence at the lunar south pole. The invitation explicitly references Chandrayaan-3's August 2023 landing near Shivshakti Point as the primary technical credential establishing India's capability to deliver payloads to the lunar south polar region — the same zone where Phase One of the Lunar Base Programme targeting 2026 through 2029 will focus robotic precursor missions on characterising the environment, testing in-situ resource utilisation technologies, and scouting infrastructure deployment sites ahead of the first crewed surface operations planned for Phase Two beginning in 2029. India's potential contribution portfolio under discussion includes integration of future Gaganyaan-derived crew transport capabilities, ISRO robotic landers for technology demonstration payloads, and NISAR-class radar infrastructure to support communications relay architectures enabling continuous lunar surface-to-Earth data links. The formal invitation deepens a trajectory of India-US space cooperation that already includes the jointly developed NASA-ISRO NISAR synthetic aperture radar satellite scheduled for launch later in 2026, and reflects NASA's strategy of building the broadest possible international coalition for the Artemis-era lunar economy to counter China's parallel International Lunar Research Station programme, which counts approximately seventeen partner nations.",
