        headline: "Blue Origin Confirms BE-4 Main Oxygen Valve as Root Cause of May 28 New Glenn Static-Fire Explosion and Outlines Dual-Pad Architecture at Launch Complex 36 With Horizontal Integration Future — Company Remains Committed to Return to Flight Before End of 2026 as NASA B-2 Engine Testing Agreement at Stennis Space Center Accelerates Qualification Milestones",
        body: "Blue Origin published a detailed engine resolution update on August 13, 2026, formally identifying the main oxygen valve on one of the seven BE-4 kerosene-liquid oxygen engines powering the New Glenn first-stage booster as the root cause of the catastrophic explosion that destroyed the vehicle and severely damaged its only operational launch pad at Cape Canaveral's Launch Complex 36 on May 28 — a conclusion reached through a combination of telemetry reconstruction, hardware recovery from the debris field, and metallurgical analysis of recovered valve components matching a failure mode traced to insufficient valve seat sealing under cryogenic propellant loading conditions. The company simultaneously published architectural plans for a revised LC-36 pad configuration that abandons the original vertical rocket integration approach in favour of horizontal vehicle assembly with the payload mated vertically in a final rollout step — a design choice intended to reduce structural risk to the launch tower during fuelling operations and to enable a second pad position at the site, fulfilling a long-stated Blue Origin goal of dual-pad operational flexibility that would double New Glenn's potential annual flight rate once both positions are qualified. NASA's recently formalised agreement to use Blue Origin's B-2 engine test stand at Stennis Space Center for advanced BE-4 hot-fire testing was cited in the update as a critical accelerator for engine re-qualification milestones needed to support both the New Glenn return-to-flight manifest and the BE-4 engines' continued role powering United Launch Alliance's Vulcan Centaur rocket under the National Security Space Launch programme. NASA Administrator Jared Isaacman reiterated his confidence that Blue Origin remains 'very committed' to resuming New Glenn launches before the end of calendar year 2026, though analysts noted the simultaneous workstreams of pad reconstruction, engine re-qualification, and new vehicle build represent an extraordinarily compressed timeline for the company.",
