        body: "SpaceX launched 24 Starlink broadband satellites to low Earth orbit from Space Launch Complex 4 East at Vandenberg Space Force Base at 9:46 p.m. PDT on August 11 (04:46 UTC on August 12), 2026, completing the Starlink 17-49 mission — SpaceX's 51st orbital launch from the West Coast in 2026 and the company's 94th Falcon 9 flight of the year — as the booster flew a south-southwesterly trajectory to deploy the satellites into a high-inclination polar shell serving high-latitude broadband customers globally. The Falcon 9 first stage executed a standard ascent, separation, and boostback sequence before landing on SpaceX's Pacific droneship Of Course I Still Love You stationed downrange in the ocean, with all 24 newly deployed satellites establishing contact with the Starlink ground station network and beginning autonomous orbit-raising via their krypton-fuelled hall-effect ion thrusters shortly after spacecraft separation. The overnight Vandenberg launch adds to SpaceX's extraordinary 2026 cadence as launch team attention at Kennedy Space Center increasingly focuses on the month's premier event — the Falcon Heavy liftoff of NASA's Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope targeting August 30 from Launch Complex 39A, with the three-core vehicle undergoing final pad integrated systems testing this week.",
        sourceUrl: "https://spaceflightnow.com/2026/08/11/live-coverage-spacex-to-launch-24-starlink-satellites-on-falcon-9-rocket-from-vandenberg-sfb-14/",
