        headline: "Chang'e 7 August 24 Launch Window in Jeopardy as YF-100 Engine Investigation From Yesterday's Long March 7A Explosion Expands to Long March 5 Rocket — Chinese Space Authority Evaluates Whether Mandatory Stand-Down Can Be Resolved in Time to Preserve Lunar South Pole Mission Before Backup Trajectories Lapse in October",
        body: "China's space programme confronted a grave engineering crossroads on August 12, 2026, as investigators probing yesterday's catastrophic Long March 7A failure deepened their scrutiny of the YF-100K kerosene-liquid oxygen engine cluster that powers the rocket's first stage — an engine family whose core YF-100 technology is shared with the Long March 5's four side-booster engines that are needed to propel the 8.2-tonne Chang'e 7 five-element stack toward the Moon's south pole on August 24. The China National Space Administration confirmed on August 12 that a formal investigation board has been convened and that the Long March 5 launch readiness review scheduled for the week of August 18 is under review pending preliminary findings from telemetry recovery teams working to isolate whether the anomaly originated in a specific component batch defect, a propellant loading parameter error, or a systemic design characteristic shared across the engine family. Analysts tracking China's launch manifest noted that if the investigation board cannot definitively clear the Long March 5's YF-100 engines before August 18, the August 24 launch window will almost certainly slip — with backup low-energy lunar transfer trajectories available only through mid-October before the next viable window does not open until spring 2027, adding enormous schedule pressure to the programme. The incident is especially consequential given that Chang'e 7 carries all five elements of China's lunar south pole water-ice detection campaign — the relay satellite, orbiter, lander, rover, and the world's first lunar-hopping mini-probe — meaning there is no partial-mission alternative if the integrated stack cannot launch within the remaining 2026 window.",
