        headline: "SpaceX Launches 29 Starlink V2 Mini Optimized Satellites on Starlink 10-19 Mission From Cape Canaveral at 10:26 a.m. EDT After Monday Night Scrub — 93rd Falcon 9 Flight of 2026 Deploys Next-Generation Broadband Batch as Constellation Surpasses 10,950 Active Satellites Ahead of Month's Marquee Roman Space Telescope Falcon Heavy Launch on August 30",
        body: "SpaceX successfully launched 29 Starlink V2 Mini Optimized broadband satellites to low Earth orbit from Space Launch Complex 40 at Cape Canaveral Space Force Station at 10:26 a.m. EDT (14:26 UTC) on August 11, 2026, completing the Starlink 10-19 mission one day after a Monday scrub caused by unfavourable upper-level wind conditions near the booster recovery zone in the Atlantic Ocean, with the 45th Weather Squadron reporting 85 percent favourable conditions at the Tuesday window opening. The Falcon 9 first-stage booster executed a clean ascent and stage separation sequence before performing its boostback, entry, and landing burns to touch down on SpaceX's Atlantic droneship recovery vessel, extending the booster's career while all 29 newly deployed satellites established contact with the global ground station network within approximately 70 minutes of deployment and began autonomous orbit-raising to their assigned shell. Starlink 10-19 was the 93rd Falcon 9 launch of 2026 and the 72nd dedicated Starlink mission of the year, continuing SpaceX's relentless high-cadence constellation build-out as the active Starlink network surpasses 10,950 operational satellites serving more than four million broadband subscribers across all six inhabited continents. With the Starlink 10-19 mission complete, SpaceX's August manifest now pivots to the month's centrepiece event — the Falcon Heavy liftoff carrying NASA's Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope from Launch Complex 39A at Kennedy Space Center targeting August 30, 2026.",
