        body: "China's Long March 7A rocket suffered a catastrophic in-flight failure approximately 85 seconds after liftoff from the Wenchang Space Launch Center on Hainan island on August 11, 2026, erupting into a fireball that destroyed the ChinaSat-4B communications satellite it was carrying — marking the second major orbital launch failure for China in 2026 and only the second mission loss in the Long March 7A's operational history since its troubled maiden flight in 2020. The China National Space Administration confirmed the launch failure shortly after the event, stating the rocket experienced a 'flight anomaly' without providing initial details on the source of the malfunction, as investigation teams mobilised to recover telemetry data and wreckage from the South China Sea splashdown exclusion zones that had been declared before the launch. The loss of ChinaSat-4B represents a significant setback for China Satellite Communications, the state-owned operator providing broadcast and broadband relay services across the Asia-Pacific region, and the failure arrives at a particularly tense moment for the Chinese space programme with the flagship Chang'e 7 five-element lunar mission just 13 days from its planned August 24 Long March 5 liftoff — a separate rocket type — from the same Wenchang facility. Space industry analysts noted the failure will direct intense scrutiny toward the YF-100K kerosene-liquid oxygen engine cluster that powers the Long March 7A first stage, a derivative of the YF-100 engine family used across multiple Chinese launch vehicles, as engineers work to establish whether any common-cause risk could affect the broader Chinese launch manifest.",
