        body: "Europe's Ariane 6 heavy-lift rocket successfully delivered 36 Amazon Project Kuiper broadband satellites to low Earth orbit on August 11, 2026, flying in an upgraded booster configuration featuring four solid strap-on boosters each carrying 14 additional tonnes of propellant compared to the baseline variant — a significant performance enhancement that expands the rocket's LEO payload capacity and underlines Arianespace's growing commercial competitiveness as it builds toward the next launch, the Meteosat Third Generation-Imager 2 meteorological satellite, scheduled for August 27. The Ariane 6 commercial success arrives as ESA's Solar Orbiter spacecraft entered the final full day of its planned five-day dedicated pre-eclipse coronal observation campaign on August 11, with the spacecraft's Extreme Ultraviolet Imager, SPICE spectrograph, and Polarimetric and Helioseismic Imager completing the final sequences of high-cadence coronal imagery and magnetic field measurements that will form the primary observational baseline against which tomorrow's 'virtual totality' magnetohydrodynamic simulation predictions are validated. ESA's live eclipse broadcast team confirmed on-site readiness at the Observatorio Astrofísico de Javalambre in Teruel, Spain on August 11, with Dame Dr. Maggie Aderin-Pocock confirmed as host for the agency's coverage beginning at 19:30 CEST on August 12 — a broadcast incorporating real-time telescope feeds, Solar Orbiter data visualisations, and Proba-3 coronagraph imagery expected to attract the largest European space agency eclipse audience in history. The dual achievement of an Ariane 6 launch success and the culmination of Solar Orbiter's most intensive remote-sensing campaign makes August 11 one of the most productive single days in the European Space Agency's recent history across both commercial launch services and fundamental solar physics.",
