        category: "Launch",
        headline: "SpaceX Launches 29 Starlink Satellites From Cape Canaveral on August 10 as Veteran Booster Completes Its 18th Mission — Falcon 9 First Stage Lands on Droneship A Shortfall of Gravitas in the Atlantic After Clean Ascent From SLC-40, Extending Constellation to Over 10,950 Active Satellites With Roman Space Telescope Falcon Heavy Launch Just 20 Days Away",
        body: "SpaceX launched 29 Starlink broadband satellites to low Earth orbit from Space Launch Complex 40 at Cape Canaveral Space Force Station at 12:20 p.m. EDT (16:20 UTC) on August 10, 2026, continuing the company's relentless orbital internet build-out with another flawless Falcon 9 flight that extends Starlink's position as the world's largest commercial satellite network serving more than four million subscribers across six inhabited continents. The Falcon 9 first-stage booster flew for the 18th time — a veteran vehicle whose distinguished career spans Crew-9, Firefly Blue Ghost Mission 1, Fram2, SXM-10, MTG-S1, EchoStar XXV, SXM-11, and nine prior Starlink missions — before separating and landing on the droneship A Shortfall of Gravitas stationed in the Atlantic Ocean approximately eight minutes and 30 seconds after liftoff. All 29 newly deployed satellites established contact with SpaceX's global ground station network within approximately 70 minutes of deployment, before initiating autonomous orbit-raising burns to their assigned shells using krypton-fuelled hall-effect ion thrusters. The August 10 mission keeps SpaceX's summer launch cadence strong as the company prepares for the month's marquee event — the Falcon Heavy liftoff of NASA's Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope from Launch Complex 39A at Kennedy Space Center targeting August 30, 2026.",
        sourceUrl: "https://spaceflightnow.com/2026/08/10/live-coverage-spacex-to-launch-29-starlink-satellites-on-falcon-9-rocket-from-cape-canaveral-16/",
        timestamp: "2026-08-10T16:20:00Z"
