        headline: "Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope Countdown Reaches T-20 Days as Falcon Heavy Pad Rollout Scheduled for Mid-August Week Ahead of August 30 Launch to Sun-Earth L2 — Science Operations Team at STScI Stands Ready With All First-Year Programs Locked as Roman Prepares to Survey One Billion Galaxies and Transform Cosmological Measurements of Dark Energy",
        body: "NASA's Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope entered T-20 days in its countdown to the August 30 liftoff aboard a SpaceX Falcon Heavy from Launch Complex 39A at Kennedy Space Center, with the integrated Falcon Heavy vehicle scheduled for pad rollout during the week of August 11 ahead of an integrated systems test confirming end-to-end launch vehicle and observatory readiness before the final pre-launch countdown begins. The Roman observatory — encapsulated inside its Falcon Heavy payload fairing following the July 2026 fuelling and encapsulation operations — continues to return all-nominal health telemetry through umbilical monitoring connections as ground crews complete closeout inspections of the three-core Falcon Heavy vehicle and pad support systems. The Space Telescope Science Institute science operations team in Baltimore has formally locked all first-year observing programs, including the High Latitude Wide Area Survey targeting dark energy weak gravitational lensing signals across billions of faint galaxies, the Galactic Bulge Time Domain Survey designed to detect exoplanet microlensing events, and the Coronagraphic Instrument technology demonstration campaign for direct imaging of nearby giant exoplanets — a scientific programme beginning approximately 90 days after launch following telescope commissioning at the L2 Lagrange point. With its 2.4-metre mirror and 288-megapixel Wide Field Instrument delivering a field of view at least 100 times larger than Hubble per pointing, Roman is designed to survey approximately one billion galaxies over a six-year primary mission, representing the most ambitious cosmological survey ever conducted from space and NASA's largest observatory deployment since the James Webb Space Telescope reached L2 in January 2022.",
