        headline: "ESA's Solar Orbiter Begins Dedicated Five-Day Pre-Eclipse Coronal Observation Campaign on August 9 as Total Solar Eclipse Countdown Enters Four-Day Stretch — Spacecraft Targets Helmet Streamer Belt, Polar Plume Structures, and Coronal Mass Ejection Source Regions With High-Cadence Remote-Sensing Suite to Power 'Virtual Totality' Forecast Validation Experiment on August 12",
        body: "ESA's Solar Orbiter spacecraft initiated its planned five-day dedicated remote-sensing observation campaign on August 9, 2026, beginning an intensive sequence of high-cadence imaging and spectroscopic measurements of the Sun's outer corona, helmet streamer belt, and polar plume structures that will serve as the primary observational foundation for the agency's landmark 'virtual totality' experiment when the August 12 total solar eclipse traces its path across Greenland, Iceland, and mainland Spain. The campaign activates Solar Orbiter's full suite of remote-sensing instruments including the Extreme Ultraviolet Imager, the Spectral Imaging of the Coronal Environment spectrograph, and the Polarimetric and Helioseismic Imager operating at reduced telemetry cadence, with ESA's Solar and Heliospheric Observatory providing parallel white-light coronagraph coverage to deliver the most comprehensive multi-vantage-point pre-eclipse coronal dataset assembled for any European eclipse in the modern space era. The 'virtual totality' validation exercise compares the outputs of ESA's state-of-the-art magnetohydrodynamic simulation codes — pre-computed from weeks of Solar Orbiter magnetogram inputs — against ground-based coronagraph and unfiltered white-light imagery collected by scientific expedition teams already deployed along the path of totality, directly quantifying current forecast model accuracy for operational space weather purposes. With the eclipse just 72 hours away, ESA is encouraging observers across Europe and the North Atlantic to join the agency's coordinated citizen science programme and tune into the live broadcast from the Observatorio Astrofísico de Javalambre in Teruel, Spain starting at 19:30 CEST on August 12 — the culmination of a scientific campaign months in the making.",
