        body: "SpaceX confirmed on August 9, 2026, that Starship Flight 14 — the vehicle's first planned fully orbital mission — remains on track for a late-August liftoff window from Starbase in Boca Chica, Texas, with the primary outstanding milestone being FAA recertification of the flight termination system following the resolution of the propellant pressurisation valve anomaly that triggered the post-ignition abort of Starship Flight 13 on July 16 and was subsequently validated through engine test firings in late July and early August. Ship 41 is fully stacked atop Booster 21 on the orbital launch mount at Starbase, with SpaceX engineers completing final closeout inspections and vehicle health verifications as the company awaits the agency's green light following the submission of updated flight safety documentation that reflects the valve timing sequence modifications implemented after the Flight 13 root cause investigation. The Flight 14 mission profile calls for Booster 21 to execute a standard ascent and separation sequence before returning to a Mechazilla tower catch attempt at Starbase — a recovery method successfully demonstrated on three prior booster flights — while Ship 41 continues on an orbital trajectory, deploys an initial batch of next-generation Starlink V3 satellites featuring enhanced inter-satellite laser links and wider-spectrum phased arrays, and then performs a controlled atmospheric reentry over the Indian Ocean before descending for the first-ever Mechazilla catch attempt of an orbital Starship upper stage. CEO Elon Musk reiterated on August 9 via social media that the late-August window remains the target pending regulatory approval, and independent analysts monitoring FAA docket activity have noted the absence of new public comment periods as a positive signal that the agency's review is in its final stages rather than still in open consultation — though the final issuance timeline remains at FAA's discretion and could compress or extend the remaining pre-launch window.",
