        headline: "Chang'e 7 Countdown Reaches T-15 Days as All Five Spacecraft Elements Confirmed Fully Nominal at Wenchang Space Launch Center on August 9 — Mission Controllers Complete Final Payload Health Sweeps While Long March 5 Enters Terminal Pre-Launch Readiness Phase Targeting August 24 Liftoff for Lunar South Pole in China's Most Scientifically Complex Robotic Lunar Mission",
        body: "China's Chang'e 7 mission team confirmed on August 9, 2026, that the countdown to the August 24 Long March 5 liftoff from Wenchang Space Launch Center has reached the T-15 day milestone with all five spacecraft elements — the relay satellite, orbiter, lander, surface rover, and the pioneering hopping mini-probe — returning fully nominal telemetry from their integrated configuration atop the sealed launch vehicle, as the Beijing Aerospace Control Center completed the latest scheduled payload health sweep across all 21 scientific instruments including the six internationally contributed sensors from France, Switzerland, Russia, and Saudi Arabia. The Long March 5 ground support team has transitioned into terminal pre-launch readiness operations, focusing on final vehicle structural inspections, propellant system verifications, and pad facility readiness confirmations ahead of the cryogenic propellant loading operations scheduled to begin in the days before the August 24 launch window, which opens a low-energy transfer trajectory to the lunar south pole optimised for the mission's fuel-constrained landing sequence in Shackleton Crater's vicinity. Mission planners at the China Academy of Space Technology briefed the scientific community on August 9 via a published technical update confirming that the hopping mini-probe's electrostatic adhesion leg qualification campaign completed successfully in late July — validating the leg deployment mechanism through more than 150 simulated landing cycles on lunar regolith simulant at cryogenic temperatures — clearing the last major engineering milestone for the payload that has drawn the most international scientific attention as the world's first spacecraft capable of physically entering permanently shadowed crater interiors. The 15-day pre-launch period will include final propellant topping operations, engineering team readiness reviews, and a Launch Readiness Review expected in the week of August 18 — after which the mission profile anticipates a roughly five-day transfer to lunar orbit before the highly choreographed multi-element deployment sequence begins the definitive search for extractable water ice at the Moon's south pole.",
