        body: "SpaceX successfully launched the Starlink Group 17-38 mission from Space Launch Complex 4 East at Vandenberg Space Force Base in California at 14:00 UTC on August 8, 2026, deploying 24 Starlink V2 Mini broadband satellites into low Earth orbit after the mission was delayed twice — on August 6 and August 7 — due to unfavourable upper-level wind conditions over the booster recovery zone in the Pacific Ocean. The Falcon 9 Block 5 first stage, flying as booster B1093 on its 16th mission, completed a clean ascent burn before separating and executing boostback and entry burns before touching down approximately eight minutes and thirty seconds after liftoff on the droneship Of Course I Still Love You stationed in the Pacific, extending B1093's career landing streak. All 24 newly deployed Starlink V2 Mini satellites established contact with SpaceX's global ground station network within approximately 70 minutes of deployment and began autonomous orbit-raising to their assigned shells in the Starlink Group 17 plane configuration, continuing the commercial internet constellation's steady expansion past 10,900 active satellites serving subscribers across all six inhabited continents. The August 8 mission maintains SpaceX's August launch cadence as the company builds toward the month's marquee event — the Falcon Heavy liftoff of NASA's Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope from Launch Complex 39A at Kennedy Space Center targeting August 30, 2026.",
