        headline: "Blue Origin CEO Dave Limp Confirms Faulty Main Oxygen Valve on Single BE-4 Engine Caused May 28 New Glenn Explosion During Ground Test at Cape Canaveral — Modified Valve Hardware Expected Ready by End of August With Return-to-Flight Targeting Before Year's End; ULA Confirms Vulcan Centaur Fleet Will Require Same Fix at Decatur Factory and Launch Pad",
        body: "Blue Origin CEO Dave Limp publicly confirmed that the May 28, 2026 explosion that destroyed a New Glenn first stage during a routine static fire test at Cape Canaveral Space Force Station has been definitively traced to an anomaly originating at the main oxygen valve on one of the seven BE-4 engines powering the first stage — a finding backed by extensive recovered hardware inspections and component-level analysis conducted over the ten weeks following the incident. The failed valve allowed an uncontrolled oxygen-rich condition to develop that initiated rapid unplanned disassembly of the engine bay and subsequently the entire first stage, destroying the rocket and causing significant structural damage to the launch facility infrastructure that has been undergoing concurrent repairs as the investigation proceeded. Blue Origin engineers have since conducted component-level and engine hot-fire tests to fully characterise the failure mode and validate proposed design changes, and Limp stated the company is manufacturing modified valve hardware expected to be ready by the end of August 2026 — retaining a return-to-flight target before December 31, 2026, though independent analysts have expressed skepticism about the pad-rebuilding timetable. United Launch Alliance separately confirmed the same modification will be required for BE-4 engines installed in its Vulcan Centaur fleet, with Blue Origin scheduled to perform the retrofits at ULA's Decatur, Alabama manufacturing facility and at Cape Canaveral, aligning Vulcan's return-to-flight schedule with the completion of those engine modifications.",
