        body: "NASA's Nancy Grace Roman Space Telescope countdown reached the T-22 day milestone on August 8, 2026, with the integrated Falcon Heavy vehicle — carrying the sealed Roman observatory in its payload fairing — scheduled to roll out to the launch pad at Complex 39A at Kennedy Space Center in the week of August 11 for an integrated systems test before the August 30 liftoff at 07:26 a.m. EDT. The observatory, fuelled with hydrazine in July 2026 and subsequently encapsulated within its SpaceX fairing, continues to return all-nominal health telemetry through continuous umbilical monitoring connections as ground crews complete final pre-rollout inspections of the Falcon Heavy's three-core vehicle structure. The Space Telescope Science Institute's science operations team has formally locked all first-year observing programs, including the High Latitude Wide Area Survey for dark energy weak gravitational lensing measurements, the Galactic Bulge Time Domain Survey targeting exoplanet microlensing events, and the Coronagraphic Instrument technology demonstration for direct imaging of nearby giant exoplanets — a scientific programme that will begin executing approximately 90 days after launch following telescope commissioning at the Sun-Earth L2 Lagrange point one million miles from Earth. With its 2.4-metre mirror and 288-megapixel Wide Field Instrument providing a field of view at least 100 times larger than Hubble per pointing, Roman is designed to survey up to a billion galaxies over a planned six-year primary mission, transforming cosmological measurements of dark energy and establishing the most comprehensive exoplanet demographics census ever conducted from space.",
