        body: "Blue Origin has closed its first-ever external funding round, raising $10 billion at a $130 billion pre-money valuation in a transaction that ends the company's quarter-century run as an exclusively Bezos-funded venture and for the first time brings institutional investors into the ownership structure of the world's second-largest private space company. The round is anchored by a $4 billion commitment from Coatue Management alongside a $2 billion personal contribution from founder Jeff Bezos, with the remaining $4 billion drawn from additional unnamed institutional investors who competed for allocation amid strong demand according to sources familiar with the deal. The capital arrives at a critical juncture for the company: with Launch Complex 36 still under repair following the catastrophic May 28 New Glenn static fire explosion, Blue Origin must finance a return-to-flight campaign targeting December 2026 using a redesigned mobile-crane launch architecture, while simultaneously sustaining seven Blue Moon lunar lander vehicles in production to meet its NASA Artemis contract obligations and expanding New Shepard's commercial human spaceflight manifests. NASA Administrator Jared Isaacman publicly endorsed the company's recovery progress at a congressional hearing last week, describing Blue Origin as 'very committed' to resuming launches before year's end.",
        sourceUrl: "https://techcrunch.com/2026/07/08/blue-origin-reportedly-raising-10b-at-130b-valuation/",
