        agency: "SpaceX",
        category: "Launch",
        headline: "Starship Flight 13 Scrubbed Seconds Before Liftoff on July 16 as Four Raptor Engines Fail to Ignite During Super Heavy Startup Sequence — Elon Musk Confirms Two Engines Must Be Replaced Before Next Attempt, Now Targeting Early Next Week as First Operational Starlink V3 Satellite Payload Waits Aboard Ship 40 at Starbase",
        body: "SpaceX aborted the launch of Starship Flight 13 seconds before the scheduled 6:45 p.m. EDT liftoff on July 16, 2026, when automated systems detected that at least four Raptor engines on the Super Heavy booster failed to ignite during the startup sequence — triggering an automatic abort that prevented the most consequential Starship flight attempt to date from leaving the pad. CEO Elon Musk confirmed via X shortly after the scrub that the root cause was an engine-ignition anomaly requiring the removal and replacement of two specific Raptor engines from the Super Heavy booster, a procedure he estimated would most likely enable a new launch attempt in early the following week. The scrubbed mission would have been the first Starship flight to carry operational commercial cargo — 20 next-generation Starlink V3 satellites representing the first broadband satellites ever deployed from a Starship vehicle — and the flight remains critical for demonstrating heat-shield tile performance data and in-space Raptor engine-relight capabilities that NASA requires before approving Starship for crewed Artemis lunar landings. SpaceX teams at Starbase are now working through the engine swap procedure while the flight-ready Ship 40, with its V3 Starlink payload still aboard, remains at the launch site awaiting rescheduling within the active launch window.",
