        body: "Skyroot Aerospace has confirmed a July 18, 2026 launch time of 11:30 a.m. IST (06:00 UTC) for the Vikram-1 rocket's inaugural orbital mission — designated Mission Aagaman, meaning Arrival in Sanskrit — setting up what would be a landmark event in Indian commercial space history as the first attempt by a privately built and financed Indian rocket to place a payload in Earth orbit, independent of any government-supplied launch vehicle. The 24-metre, three-stage solid-fuelled rocket with a liquid orbital adjustment module is carrying five payloads including technology demonstration cubesats from Grahaa Space, Cosmoserve, and DCubed, Skyroot's own SCOPE telemetry package, and an artistic payload called Cosmic Bloom — a rideshare lineup that reflects the commercial model Skyroot intends to operate at scale once Vikram-1 is proven. Vikram-1's July 18 window is the seventh day of a 24-day launch campaign that opened July 12 and runs through August 4, with all vehicle systems reporting nominal status as of July 17 pre-launch reviews and the First Launch Complex at SDSC-SHAR configured for the attempt following IN-SPACe range safety approval. Success would confirm India as the third country after the United States and China to achieve privately developed orbital launch capability and would provide a proof-of-concept for the broader cohort of Indian commercial launch developers — including Agnikul Cosmos, Bellatrix Aerospace, and PierSat — building their own rockets under the same IN-SPACe framework that Skyroot helped pioneer.",
