        headline: "Blue Origin Closes $10 Billion Fundraise at $130 Billion Pre-Money Valuation in Company's First-Ever External Capital Round — Coatue Asset Management Leads at $4 Billion While Jeff Bezos Commits $2 Billion; Proceeds Fuel New Glenn Return-to-Flight Campaign With New Hybrid Crane-Erect Launch Architecture Targeting End-of-2026 First Mission After LC-36 Explosion",
        body: "Blue Origin has closed its first-ever external fundraising round, raising $10 billion at a $130 billion pre-money valuation from a consortium led by Coatue Asset Management — contributing approximately $4 billion — alongside a $2 billion personal commitment from founder Jeff Bezos and additional participation from other institutional investors, transforming the company from an exclusively Bezos-bankrolled private venture into a more conventionally financed aerospace enterprise. The round, reported July 8 and confirmed across multiple financial outlets, followed SpaceX's prior-month IPO in which SpaceX raised more than $85 billion at a $1.75 trillion valuation — an event that recalibrated institutional assumptions about commercial space returns and accelerated Blue Origin's access to outside capital. Proceeds are expected to support the company's accelerated return-to-flight campaign following the May 28, 2026, explosion at Launch Complex 36 at Cape Canaveral, which destroyed Blue Origin's sole active New Glenn launch pad and forced indefinite postponement of the 2026 commercial manifest — including satellites for Amazon's Project Kuiper and AST SpaceMobile — with CEO Dave Limp now targeting at least one New Glenn flight before year-end using a newly designed hybrid architecture that assembles the rocket off-pad, transports it to the launch site, and erects it with a crane before attaching the customer payload. The $130 billion valuation reflects investor confidence in Blue Origin's long-range portfolio spanning New Glenn commercial launch, the Blue Moon lunar lander under NASA contract, and orbital manufacturing ambitions in a space economy analysts project in the trillions.",
        sourceUrl: "https://www.cnbc.com/2026/07/08/blue-origin-bezos-fundraising.html",
