        headline: "SpaceX Crosses 600th Falcon Booster Reuse Milestone With July 13 Starlink 10-45 Mission Flown by Booster B1080 on Its 28th Flight From Cape Canaveral SLC-40 — Predawn Launch Delivers 29 Starlink Satellites Before B1080 Lands on Droneship A Shortfall of Gravitas for the 638th Total Booster Recovery in Company History, Underscoring Reusability Programme's Unprecedented Scale",
        body: "SpaceX crossed a landmark reusability milestone in the early hours of July 13, 2026, when Falcon 9 booster B1080 lifted off from Space Launch Complex 40 at Cape Canaveral Space Force Station at 5:10 a.m. EDT on the Starlink 10-45 mission, becoming the 600th individual reuse of a flight-proven Falcon first stage before landing approximately eight and a half minutes later on the droneship A Shortfall of Gravitas — the 638th total booster recovery in SpaceX's operating history. The distinction between the 600th reuse milestone and the 638th landing tally reflects that some recovered boosters are held in reserve or undergoing extended maintenance rather than immediately reflown, with the reuse count tracking specifically the number of times a previously landed stage has been cleared, refurbished, and returned to active service under SpaceX's rapid-turnaround protocols. B1080's 28th flight delivered 29 next-generation Starlink broadband satellites to low Earth orbit, continuing the constellation build-out alongside a simultaneous Vandenberg launch of Starlink 15-14 with 27 additional satellites on a separate booster — a dual-coast cadence that illustrates how comprehensively reusability has reshaped the company's operational tempo since the first Falcon booster was reflown in March 2017. The 600th reuse figure encapsulates a transformation from a commercially unproven concept nine years ago to the standard economic assumption underpinning every SpaceX pricing model and, increasingly, every launch-market competitor's strategic planning horizon.",
        sourceUrl: "https://spaceflightnow.com/2026/07/13/live-coverage-spacex-to-launch-a-flight-proven-falcon-booster-for-600th-time/",
