        body: "Blue Origin CEO Dave Limp has confirmed that engineering and operations teams are maintaining an accelerated around-the-clock work schedule aimed at restoring New Glenn launch capability following the May 28, 2026, explosion at Launch Complex 36 at Cape Canaveral Space Force Station, which heavily damaged the company's sole active New Glenn pad and forced the indefinite postponement of the entire 2026 launch manifest at a moment when New Glenn was beginning to establish a commercial track record after its successful inaugural flight in January 2025. Independent engineering assessments indicate that full structural repair and recertification of LC-36 could require as long as 12 months, prompting Blue Origin to simultaneously evaluate the use of alternate Cape Canaveral launch infrastructure as a near-term bridge strategy intended to allow at least one New Glenn mission to fly before the end of calendar year 2026 — preserving commercial customer relationships and NASA programme timelines at risk from prolonged launch unavailability. NASA officials separately stated confidence in a New Glenn return to flight by end-2026 or early 2027, a window that keeps manifested science payloads viable but leaves almost no margin for Blue Origin to approach the twelve-launch-per-year cadence originally planned for 2026 as a competitive counter to SpaceX's Falcon 9 and ULA's Vulcan Centaur. The New Glenn vehicle fleet itself was confirmed unaffected by the explosion and is in storage ready for processing, meaning the return-to-flight timeline is constrained by pad availability and regulatory re-approval rather than vehicle damage or hardware replacement.",
