        category: "Launch",
        headline: "SpaceX Launches Falcon 9 Booster B1071 on 35th Flight From Vandenberg Space Force Base Deploying 24 Starlink V2 Mini Optimized Satellites to Polar Low Earth Orbit on Starlink 17-48 Mission, Booster Lands on Pacific Drone Ship Of Course I Still Love You — Second Vandenberg Starlink Mission in 24 Hours Continues Record-Breaking 2026 Cadence as Constellation Satellite Count Approaches 15,000-Satellite Milestone",
        body: "SpaceX launched a Falcon 9 rocket from Space Launch Complex 4E at Vandenberg Space Force Base on the evening of July 11, 2026, delivering 24 Starlink V2 Mini Optimized broadband satellites to polar low Earth orbit on the Starlink 17-48 mission, with first-stage booster B1071 completing its 35th flight and returning to land approximately eight minutes after liftoff on the Of Course I Still Love You autonomous drone ship positioned in the Pacific Ocean. The mission continues SpaceX's relentless high-inclination Starlink deployment pace from Vandenberg, which serves as the primary site for polar-orbit batches supporting maritime, aviation, and high-latitude subscribers in the Arctic, Antarctic, and circumpolar routes where earlier-launched mid-inclination Starlink shells offer degraded geometry. B1071's 35th flight joins the growing fleet of high-cycle-count Falcon 9 boosters that SpaceX has accumulated since first reusing a booster in March 2017, each reuse mission reducing effective per-kilogram launch costs and demonstrating the rapid-refurbishment economics that underpin SpaceX's launch-market dominance. With 24 satellites per polar-orbit mission and multiple active launch pads running simultaneously on both coasts, the Starlink constellation's satellite count continues climbing toward the next major operational milestone of 15,000 satellites, a threshold at which per-subscriber network capacity is projected to roughly triple relative to initial deployment densities.",
        sourceUrl: "https://spaceflightnow.com/2026/07/10/live-coverage-spacex-to-launch-24-starlink-satellites-on-falcon-9-rocket-from-vandenberg-sfb-10/",
