        body: "Chinese commercial launch company Orienspace conducted the second flight of its Gravity-1 solid-propellant rocket on July 11, 2026, lifting off from a repurposed cargo vessel at the Haiyang Offshore launch site in the Yellow Sea and successfully deploying three commercial earth-observation satellites to sun-synchronous orbit, extending the company's record as operator of the world's most powerful solid-fuel orbital launch vehicle capable of delivering up to 6.5 tonnes to low Earth orbit or 4.2 tonnes to sun-synchronous orbit. The Gravity-1 rocket made its debut on January 11, 2024, in a mission that successfully deployed satellites and cemented Orienspace's position among China's emerging tier of private launch providers alongside earlier commercial entrants Landspace — whose Zhuque-2 became the first methane-fuelled rocket to reach orbit in July 2023 — and other commercial operators now active in the Chinese market. Sea-based launches from modified cargo vessels allow Chinese commercial companies to select optimal azimuths for sun-synchronous and polar orbit insertion without the trajectory constraints imposed by the coastal geography of fixed onshore spaceports, a capability that mirrors offshore launch approaches historically employed by Sea Launch and more recently by multiple Chinese competitors testing maritime launch architectures in the Yellow Sea and Bohai Bay. The successful second Gravity-1 flight reinforces China's accelerating commercial launch ecosystem, which in 2025 and 2026 has delivered first orbital flights from multiple new private entrants while collectively expanding the national launch manifest well beyond what state operators CASC and CASIC could sustain on their own.",
