        body: "India's Skyroot Aerospace opens its official launch window for the Vikram-1 Mission Aagaman on July 12, 2026, targeting liftoff from the First Launch Complex at the Satish Dhawan Space Centre in Sriharikota — a window extending through August 4 — with a successful orbital insertion set to make Vikram-1 the first privately developed Indian rocket to reach orbit and mark a pivotal milestone in India's commercial space liberalisation under the IN-SPACe licensing and facilitation framework established in 2020 to open state infrastructure to private operators. The Vikram-1 rocket is a three-stage vehicle with a liquid-fuelled first and second stage and a solid-propellant kick stage, built from an all-carbon-composite airframe and incorporating partially 3D-printed engine components developed in-house at Skyroot's Hyderabad facility, with a dedicated payload capacity of approximately 480 kilograms to low Earth orbit — competitive with international small-lift vehicles Vikram-1 will face in the commercial market. Mission Aagaman carries three customer cubesats chosen to demonstrate reliable low-cost orbit access for the growing domestic and international small-satellite community that has historically relied on ISRO's PSLV rideshare or foreign launch providers such as SpaceX Transporter, and a successful flight would allow Skyroot to begin commercial operations from the same Sriharikota spaceport facilities that have served India's national programme since 1971. Vikram-1 follows the company's successful Vikram-S suborbital demonstration in November 2022 — the first flight of any privately built Indian rocket — and industry observers regard this debut orbital attempt as a watershed moment analogous to SpaceX's Falcon 1 first successful orbital flight in September 2008, which similarly transformed investor and government confidence in the private launch sector.",
