        headline: "China's Tianwen-2 Completes Third Orbit-Lowering Burn at Asteroid Kamoʻoalewa and Begins Touch-and-Go Rehearsal Sequence Preparation as Fourth Image Batch Reveals Boulder-Free Southern Equatorial Candidate Site — Mission Team Schedules First Proximity Descent Simulations Before End of July Ahead of August Sample Collection Attempt as Scientists Debate Lunar Fragment Versus Chondritic Composition Hypotheses",
        body: "China's Tianwen-2 spacecraft has completed its third orbit-lowering manoeuvre at near-Earth asteroid 469219 Kamoʻoalewa, descending to within 10 kilometres of the asteroid's surface and releasing a fourth batch of high-resolution imagery to mission scientists at the Chinese Academy of Sciences that reveals a previously unseen southern equatorial site characterised by metre-scale boulders interspersed with patches of fine-grained regolith — a surface texture potentially compatible with the anchor-and-attach sampling technique that Tianwen-2 is uniquely equipped to deploy. The mission team at the Beijing Aerospace Control Centre confirmed that touch-and-go rehearsal sequences — low-altitude proximity descents that simulate final approach trajectory geometry and proximity-sensor triggering without establishing surface contact — are scheduled to begin before the end of July, representing the final qualification step before the first actual sample collection attempt currently planned for August 2026. Kamoʻoalewa's 28-minute rotation period and irregular elongated shape, now well-characterised by onboard stereo camera and laser altimeter data accumulated since the spacecraft's arrival on July 6, have been incorporated into updated gravity-field and solar-radiation-pressure models that the flight dynamics team is using to design the optimal final-descent trajectory minimising the risk of non-nominal attitude at surface contact. The four image batches released since arrival have intensified scientific debate between the research teams proposing a lunar fragment origin — supported by spectroscopic similarity to lunar highland material — and those favouring an ordinary chondritic composition in the Flora asteroid family, with CNSA scientists confirming that definitive resolution awaits laboratory geochemical and isotopic analysis of the 200-to-1,000-gram sample expected to return to the Jiuquan landing zone in November 2027.",
