        body: "NASA's Swift Boost Mission — the first commercial contract in agency history to service an operational NASA science spacecraft — suffered its second consecutive weather-related scrub on July 1 as persistent unfavourable conditions over Kwajalein Atoll in the Republic of the Marshall Islands forced the Northrop Grumman Stargazer L-1011 carrier aircraft to stand down again, with teams now targeting a next launch attempt no earlier than July 2 at 5:09 a.m. EDT. The air-launch mission, which will see Katalyst Space Systems' 880-pound LINK robotic servicing spacecraft deploy from the Pegasus XL rocket and autonomously rendezvous with NASA's Neil Gehrels Swift Observatory to raise its decaying orbit by approximately 75 kilometres, first scrubbed on June 30 and must contend with narrow daily launch windows dictated by Swift's orbital geometry relative to Kwajalein's mid-Pacific location. LINK is equipped with three robotic grappling arms and ion thrusters that will fire over several months after docking with Swift — a telescope not originally designed for in-space servicing — to prevent an uncontrolled atmospheric reentry forecast for autumn 2026 and preserve the observatory's role as NASA's premier rapid-response gamma-ray burst detection platform through at least 2031. Mission weather officers and Katalyst teams are monitoring meteorological forecasts for the July 2 window as the Stargazer aircraft and ground support personnel remain on standby at Kwajalein Atoll awaiting suitable conditions.",
