        body: "NASA declared US Spacewalk 95 a full success following the June 30 extravehicular activity in which Expedition 73 flight engineers Chris Williams and Jessica Meir concluded a 7-hour, 20-minute repair operation at 3:40 p.m. EDT, having completely removed the malfunctioning wrist joint on Canadarm2 and installed a pre-positioned spare unit, restoring the arm's structural integrity for the first time since it failed to respond during routine operations on May 27. Mission Control in Houston performed an initial power-up of the repaired arm immediately after the spacewalk concluded, confirming healthy electrical connectivity, data throughput, and communications between the new joint and the station's systems — marking the fourth time in Canadarm2's 25-year history that spacewalkers have performed in-person maintenance on the Canadian robotic arm. Ground controllers will spend the coming week conducting a progressive series of checkout manoeuvres, gradually commanding Canadarm2 through increasingly complex motions to validate the new joint's performance across its full operational range before certifying the arm for return to active service supporting cargo transfers, vehicle berthing, and future spacewalk staging. The repair was completed just in time for Canada Day on July 1 and underscores the ISS's modular design philosophy — the ability to replace failed components with on-orbit spares avoids the prohibitive cost of manufacturing and delivering replacement hardware from Earth.",
        sourceUrl: "https://www.nasa.gov/blogs/spacestation/2026/06/30/astronauts-repair-canadarm2-robotic-arm-and-complete-spacewalk/",
