        body: "China's Tianwen-2 asteroid sample-return spacecraft is executing its final approach to asteroid 469219 Kamoʻoalewa as of July 1, 2026, with mission controllers at the Beijing Aerospace Control Centre guiding the probe toward a milestone close encounter around July 4 at which the spacecraft is expected to reach within 20 kilometres of the quasi-moon's surface and begin systematic remote sensing characterisation ahead of the most complex phase of the mission. The spacecraft will progressively lower its altitude from 20 kilometres through intermediate survey orbits at 3 kilometres, 600 metres, and finally 300 metres, using its suite of spectrometers, cameras, and laser altimeters to map Kamoʻoalewa's topography, rotation state, surface composition, and regolith properties in sufficient detail to select the safest and scientifically richest site for touchdown and sample collection of at least 100 grams. Kamoʻoalewa is an unusual quasi-satellite of Earth that orbits the Sun in a resonant pattern keeping it perpetually near our planet, and a leading hypothesis among planetary scientists holds that it is a fragment ejected from the Moon during an ancient giant impact — making the mission's sample a potential window into the deep history of the Earth-Moon system that no other mission has yet opened. Tianwen-2 will attempt both an anchor-and-attach touchdown — the first application of this technique on any asteroid — and a backup touch-and-go collection, before sealing samples in a hermetic return capsule targeted for delivery to Earth near Jiuquan in November 2027.",
