        body: "The Indian Space Research Organisation has confirmed the launch of its Geo Imaging Satellite GISAT-1A (EOS-05) aboard a GSLV Mk II rocket designated GSLV-F18 for July 31, 2026, from the Second Launch Pad at the Satish Dhawan Space Centre in Sriharikota, in what represents the agency's most significant upcoming orbital mission following a period of setbacks that prompted wide-ranging reliability reviews across India's launch vehicle portfolio. GISAT-1A is the replacement successor to the original GISAT-1 lost in August 2021 when the cryogenic upper stage failed to ignite, and carries design modifications intended to eliminate the failure mode while retaining the satellite's capability to deliver near-real-time optical imagery of the Indian subcontinent and surrounding region from geostationary orbit at approximately 36,000 kilometres altitude. The mission is also proceeding alongside a national-level expert committee review constituted to investigate the root causes of two consecutive PSLV third-stage anomalies — the PSLV-C61 failure in May 2025 that destroyed the EOS-09 radar satellite and the PSLV-C62 failure in January 2026 that claimed the EOS-N1 Anvesha DRDO satellite plus 15 co-passenger CubeSats — with ISRO implementing stringent additional verification protocols across all launch vehicle systems pending the committee's findings. A successful GISAT-1A mission would mark a confidence-restoring milestone for India's space programme and validate the long-delayed cryogenic upper stage improvements that underpin ISRO's ambitions for heavier payloads under the Gaganyaan crewed programme and future deep-space missions.",
